{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/lunar-exalted/1175049-what-have-you-used-the-canon-ancient-lunars-for-in-your-games","id":1175049,"title":"What have you used the canon ancient Lunars for in your games?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted","Lunar Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-01-10T18:25:33-08:00","id":1175049,"title":"What have you used the canon ancient Lunars for in your games?","content":"I'm thinking about Raksi and Ma Ha Suchi in particular, but I'd like to hear how you've included Leviathan et al in your stories as well. How do you characterize them? What do they bring to your game?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>When Heaven forbids Exigents, only forbidden gods will have Exigents. <b>The Hanged Man wrote:</b> Space. Pfft, whatever. I can kick Space's ass with a starting Dynast.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/716-capybara","name":"Capybara!","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar716_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-11T01:07:31-08:00","id":1175123,"title":"","content":"In my current game, set in the West, the whole party are Lunar Exalted. In my game I've divided up elders into two broad categories, Crones and Mothers, to help my players keep a better grasp on who is or isn't a two-thousand year old god-monster from the dawn of time.<br /> <br /> The only canon Elder (or 'Crone') who has gotten any play in my game is Leviathan, who I've described as being a figure of folklore throughout the islands near Sunken Luthe. He was a whale that hunted fleets a long, long time ago, but he hasn't been seen for several centuries. Most people still think he's out there, and they give Sunken Luthe a wide berth to avoid attracting his attention and the wrath that comes with it.<br /> <br /> I'm sure eventually my party will get their shit together and come knocking on old Levi's door, and when that happens they'll be in for an encounter with a sea monster given human form. <br /> <br /> Leviathan is mighty and grand in every sense of the word, swollen to immense heights by the lingering traces of his cetacean spirit. Barnacles dot the backs of his hands and the sides of his wrists, where the flesh is stiff and thick like bulkheads. His leathery sun-tanned skin is criss-crossed by the white lines of a thousand thousand scars inflicted by every enemy in the ocean and the Wyld beyond it. The symbol of his old station, the great longcoat that once signified him as Grand Admiral of the Solar Deliberative, hangs about his shoulders like a cape, patched with sailcloth torn from a hundred masts. Not a single coward's scar mars his back. He carries Islebreaker, his mighty three-pronged Direlance, against his shoulder like a common soldier would a spear - save for the fact that his weapon measures as long as a trireme's keel. Leviathan's face, held a height that makes it difficult to see from a man's perspective, is oddly small on his immense body. His eyes are pinpricks of gleaming light beneath a twisted brow, and his thin lips curl upwards along the line of an ancient scar.<br /> <br /> He's an intimidating figure, but he's also prone to high spirits and bold proclamations of camaraderie among Luna's children. He remembers the Deliberative and his time as it's Admiral fondly, and he wishes to one day reunite the family he lost to the fires of the Usurpation. To that end he gathers a vast crew from all walks of life across the West, with several young Lunars under his tutelage.<br /> <br /> Also, he laughs like 'gurarararara.'","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11619-kymmetheseventh","name":"KymmetheSeventh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11619_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-11T04:34:53-08:00","id":1175139,"title":"","content":"I've had them as a mixture of different things. They've been allies, villains or just interesting people to meet and interact with. I found they bring ties to the First Age. For most, they are a reminder of the Solar's faults and flaws and in some cases their direct wrong doing. They've also served as useful go to points for info and assistance. <br /> <br /> I upped Lilith's angst to the point where she created a new sect of Lunars that are basically the opposite of the Seneschals, dedicated to tracking down and killing their Solar mates to get the +1 essence from that one knack I forget the name of (golden widow method I think?). She is also the mate of one of my PC's and has tried to murder him several times. I've characterised her as super angry but sad too. They've slowly uncovered throughout the campaign the depths that Desus abused her and they feel sorry for her. To the point where they might want to actually try and bring her to their side rather than murder her next time they encounter her. <br /> <br /> Raksi was a villain but only briefly. One of my PC's is Brigid's reincarnation so really wanted the book of 3 Circles but being crazy, Raksi kidnapped him when he arrived and tried to eat his brain so she could try to figure out how to be a Solar Circle sorcerer. Was also conspiring with some Infernals as being an akuma might also have got her what she wanted. They bopped her with a hearthstone that temporarily suppresses insanity for an hour though and while returned to her senses she apologised and requested they give her a clean death before she went crazy again. So ultimately not a villain and was more her insanity that made her do bad things. <br /> <br /> Ma-Ha-Suchi is presented as an irredeemable ass hole in most of the fluff but I tried to downplay a lot of what's written. I made him angry, offensive and abrasive but not to the point of being stupid or petulant. Characters like Ma-Ha-Suchi with prejudices, I find, need to have a good estbalished justifcation for how they are. Otherwise they'll just be dismissed out of hand. He helped in a big battle when the Raksha from the North tried to invade and the Lunars had to make a brave stand at Diamond Hearth as I think he'd still fight f Currently opinion of him is he's a jerk but not a complete jerk. <br /> <br /> Leviathan I struggled with. Like all first age Lunars he's full of angst in the fluff and I had written that he was also the mate of one of my PC's who'd been a great Solar general/warrior, so seemed fitting. When they arrived in Luthe I was expecting them to fight or something, I was going to have Leviathan shout at him and stuff. But it didn't seem to fit in the moment so instead they just talked it out. I think Leviathan can be one of the friendlier ones if he's given justification or incentive. By this point in our game, the world is ending so why wouldn't he side with them? Was my thinking. I characterised him as sad at first. He had a lot of hang ups about abandoning his mate to go after his lover instead and just broke down when the PC told him there was nothing to forgive. After that he's dedicated himself to their cause and even had a friendly sparring match with the PC atop Luthe so very much into brotherly affection and comradery. <br /> <br /> Tamuz I was most interested in because of the whole, amazing strategist thing. For context in my game, the First and Forsaken Lion created a huge shadowland and marched the Legion out to attack the South. The PC's unified the South and led a massive army to counter them but Tamuz, at this point having re-donned his old persona as Tamas-Khan, said he wouldn't join them because it was hopeless but would be back up for when the Legion marched further North. However this was a ploy. He knew the Legion would position themselves differently and commit less forces if they thought there wasn't a horde of Delzahn cavalry to contend with. So when the battle came, Tamuz came sweeping in at the last second and with some more late arrivals, saved the day. However not everyone was happy with this as the rest of the South had crippled their armies and sacrificed a lot before they arrived. So I characterised him as doing the right thing but in the smartest way, long term. This didn't always make sense to the PC's who were very focused on the present but it won them the battle despite heavy casualties.<br /> <br /> Finally, Rain Deathflyer and Silver Python were just background NPC's in a Lunar gathering they attended when the Raksha invasion was about to happen. So not much to say on them. With what little I did do, I remember characterising them as fair, willing to listen to younger Lunars but I didn't downplay their age and power. I'm pretty sure I gave Silver Python the giant form knack so I could describe a massive snake wrecking shit when the gathering came under attack. <br /> <br /> Hope that helps <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4612-solar-lantern","name":"Solar Lantern","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4612_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"77"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}